00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.761
I like to improvise.


00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:24.485
I always think I like
to do things


00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.849
as though I'm...
I'm playing jazz.


00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.889
Try this, try that.


00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.167
Okay, the sneakers
from Home Shopping.


00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.243
I design them and I sell them.


00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.041
Ordinary black denim jeans.
I'm a denim freak.


00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.884
Reminded me of a railroad man.


00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.691
When I was a kid,
I was a railroad buff.


00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:47.161
These bracelets
come from Harlem.


00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:48.890
These I have such a long time,


00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:50.485
I don't even remember
where they came from,


00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.847
but they were like
slivers of black,


00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.126
so I love them.


00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:58.691
And then these are just things
I've been collecting


00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.330
over the years.


00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.407
AnyWaY-


00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:03.925
Another mad outfit.


00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:07.283
These are my shoes,
smoking slippers.


00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.410
Suede, embroidered.


00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.645
This kind of tunic
is from the Mao people,


00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.604
Chinese minority.


00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.041
It had a big hood,
and I didn't like the hood,


00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.850
so I made it into a collar.


00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.085
And this is part
of my collection of amber.


00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.971
And then bracelets
from all over the place,


00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.963
including these ladybugs.


00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.369
Okay?


00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.722
I like to put
things together like this.


00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:47.410
This is an old jacket
from, uh... Ungaro.


00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.325
I love the way it's lined.


00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.121
And these are Gianni Versace
trousers.


00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.250
So they're kind of fun together.


00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:57.721
I'll wear them
with a black turtleneck.


00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.530
Just has to feel right with me.


00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.802
Every time I do it,
I do a different way.


00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.646
I like individuality.


00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:09.362
So lost these days.
There's so much sameness.


00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.291
Everything is homogenized.
I hate it.


00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.245
Whatever.


00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.410
- Albert?
- Yes?


00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.085
Do you think we're demented?


00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.246
So easy to amuse me.


00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.449
I get more kick out of this
that costs $4 and change


00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:49.122
than if my husband took me
to Harry Winston.


00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.802
People interview me
and they keeping asking me


00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.008
about all these rules,
and I say


00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.003
I don't have any rules
because I would


00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.731
only be breaking them,
so it's a waste of time.


00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.531
With me, it's not intellectual.
It's all gut.


00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.444
I see something,
"Oh, that'll go with this."


00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:04.965
I'll try it on.


00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:06.446
It's totally,
totally the involvement


00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:08.125
and the process.


00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:09.566
It's the process
I like much better


00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:11.006
even than wearing it.


00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:14.922
What could you do
on these three pieces?


00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:17.845
Fifteen, 25, 30.


00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.411
How about $25 for all three?


00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:21.766
Can you do $20?


00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.446
Mm, all right, why not?


00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.569
I think it was
some very famous clothes horse


00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.007
said the best thing
was getting dressed.


00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:35.810
She didn't give a damn
about going to the party


00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:37.041
or being at the party.


00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.929
It was getting dressed
for the party.


00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.447
And there's more truth
and poetry in that.


00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:58.011
We are so pleased
to have Iris Apfel.


00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.642
This event will show you
how she works


00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.525
with people, their bodies,


00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.405
and the way they should look
or the way they can look.


00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:10.285
What brings you
to our fair shore?


00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.966
I want something that
represents my personality


00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:16.681
but is also classic,
so I need help.


00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:19.241
There's somebody I've had
my eye on.


00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.920
You, you, with the earrings.
Come here, girl.


00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.921
- Oh.
- Oh, wowie-wow.


00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.363
This was a Gaultier.


00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.842
I thought it needed a belt.
So we added the belt.


00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.329
The shawl is also an add-on,
but look at those shoes.


00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:40.686
She looks very Vogue-y.


00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:43.925
This was something that
when lris picked it out,


00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:45.764
I thought she was a little...


00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:47.206
I thought her 90 years
was showing.


00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.124
I never would
have worn this myself.


00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.009
I would have looked at it
on the rack and kept going.


00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:52.321
But I love it.


00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.683
This is my wild child.


00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:00.687
She told me that sometimes
she has to go to occasions


00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.241
where she has to be
a bit more sophisticated.


00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.050
And I thought,
now that's a challenge,


00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:08.926
to make her look sophisticated
and kooky at the same time


00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.122
is not the easiest thing
in the world.


00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:12.930
But we'll give it a try,


00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.122
so see if you think
we've succeeded.


00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:17.647
Francis, my gosh.


00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.409
Wow.


00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:25.049
Dress looks pure couture.


00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.521
The necklace I think
is Missoni.


00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.166
The necklace is spoken for.
I'm buying it.


00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:34.844
Okay, baby.
I think you're in.


00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:36.365
Thank you.


00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:41.721
I was very much influenced
by my mother,


00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:45.248
and my mother worshipped
at the altar of the accessory.


00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:47.243
My mother could do tricks
with a scarf


00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.402
that I've never seen
anybody else do.


00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.928
It was during Depression days
that I grew up,


00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:58.809
and my mother always said,
"Buy a good, simple


00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.604
little black dress


00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.331
and you'll always have
something to wear


00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.450
because you can dress it up
or you can dress it down."


00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.562
Thank you for sharing
your vision.


00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:14.048
And on behalf of Loehmann's
90th year,


00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:17.249
we would like to celebrate
your 90th birthday, too.


00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.007
Oh, what a pretty cake!
What a pretty cake!


00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:23.049
Oh, my God.


00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.171
Looked like the eternal flame.


00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.930
I had discovered Loehmann's,
it was in Brooklyn.


00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:45.289
That was when Mrs. Loehmann,
the original...


00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.363
who was absolutely a trip.


00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:50.091
She looked like something
out of Toulouse-Lautrec.


00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:53.084
And she would sit
on a high stool


00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:55.720
like she was observing
a tennis match.


00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:58.770
And she would look at what
everybody in the shop was doing.


00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.331
And she used to fixate on me,


00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.409
and she really made me
very uncomfortable.


00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.689
And one day, she called me over,
and she said,


00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:08.320
"Young lady,
I've been watching you."


00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:10.209
She said, "Don't anybody...


00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.966
You're not pretty,
and you'll never be pretty.


00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:15.925
But it doesn't matter.
You have something much better.


00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:17.610
You have style."


00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:23.444
Iris is an artist.


00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.444
What she uses all of her
clothing


00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:31.606
and her accessories to do
is compose a new vision.


00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:35.723
That for me is creativity.


00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:37.689
Other people would say,
"Well, you know,


00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:39.604
it's just getting dressed,
and we all get dressed."


00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:41.683
Well, we all take snapshots
on our phone,


00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:43.490
and that doesn't make it art.


00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:45.563
The fact that she
combines everything,


00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.291
what is nice, what is ugly,
what's cheap, what is chic.


00:07:48.440 --> 00:07:51.330
So all these things,
and she just mixes that,


00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.209
and she makes
her own style of that,


00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:55.966
and that's really...
that's inspiration for me.


00:07:56.160 --> 00:07:59.130
Her things are so special.


00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:01.681
Everything Mrs. Apfel have
is gorgeous.


00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:07.322
When I first met her,
there were all kinds of people,


00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.882
all ages,
all walks of life,


00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.202
all surrounding her.


00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:16.409
She was so engaging
to everybody, you know?


00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:18.967
And I could feel the pulse
of her life,


00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:21.122
of her excitement
about living.


00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.048
This is Iris Apfel,


00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.125
and she is
a legendary collector,


00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:31.846
um, of fashion.


00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:33.650
Exhibits of her fashions
in museums


00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:36.451
like the Metropolitan Museum
of Art's Costume Institute


00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:38.409
drew record crowds.


00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:40.642
And that was in 2005.
I went.


00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:42.450
It was extraordinary.


00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.568
And tell us what you're wearing
right now, Iris.


00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.564
Oh, this actually
I bought many years ago


00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:49.165
for my darling husband,
and...


00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:50.446
You bought this shirt?


00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:53.171
Yeah,
this was done by Versace.


00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:55.607
He painted it himself.
It's leather.


00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:57.325
So I grabbed it,
and I took it home.


00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.131
And I gave it to him,
and he turned up his nose.


00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:01.611
And he said, "Oh..."


00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:04.047
lfis'husband
is sitting right here.


00:09:04.200 --> 00:09:06.282
Carl is sitting right
here in the front row.


00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:10.371
And Carl has pretty
fancy pants on.


00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:11.601
Look at those pants.


00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:13.091
They're from Etro.
I bought him those.


00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.009
- Oh, are those new?
- Yeah.


00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:18.044
Oh, okay. Very nice.
You're right with it, Carl.


00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:19.446
Thank you, dear.


00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:32.168
This goes back
to when the dinosaurs


00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:35.210
were roaming the Earth,
1948.


00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:38.764
Articles from The Times
and other newspapers.


00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:44.764
This is what
I looked like then.


00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:51.603
I never wanted a wedding.


00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:53.205
I wanted to elope.


00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:55.050
I said I'd rather have
the money.


00:09:56.720 --> 00:09:59.200
But the parents
and the grandparents


00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:01.124
wanted the wedding.


00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:04.490
Our first date
was Columbus Day.


00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:07.129
Thanksgiving, Carl proposed.


00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:09.721
Christmas, I got blinged.


00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.247
Washington's birthday,
we were married.


00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:16.051
Washington's birthday,
February 22nd.


00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:18.962
There was no
Presidents' Day then.


00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:22.722
It was a fairly small wedding,
I think 125 people.


00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:25.042
But very posh.


00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:29.285
The dress was pink lace,
and I'm really very practical,


00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.801
So I wanted a dress that
I could wear after the wedding


00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:33.962
and notjust put in a box.


00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:36.327
I still have these shoes


00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:40.804
sixty...
six and a half years later


00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:43.361
They were pale pink satin.


00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:45.204
They're back in style.


00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:48.330
If you hang around long enough,
everything comes back.


00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:53.247
This is ma and pa and moi.


00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:54.970
No siblings.


00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:01.969
There's Carl.
So gallant.


00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:05.282
There was something about her


00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:08.410
that just got into me.


00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:09.966
It's always there.


00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:12.488
Aw, my little pussycat.


00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.082
And I figured he was cool
and he was cuddly,


00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:18.050
and he cooked Chinese,
so I couldn't do any better.


00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:37.086
But it's been one trip,


00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:39.049
beautiful trip.


00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:41.729
I trained him well.


00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.645
And if she wants a child,
I'm it.


00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.210
He's a handful.


00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:01.131
The first piece I ever bought
was in Greenwich Village.


00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:06.451
I was about maybe 11,
12 years old.


00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:10.043
There was a little shop
in a basement


00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:12.407
of one of those old-fashioned
kind of tenement houses


00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.131
that had
the fire escapes outside,


00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:17.650
and I'll never forget
that place


00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:20.446
because I thought
it was Aladdin's cave.


00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:21.840
There was this little man.


00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.207
His name was Mr. Darris,


00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:27.087
and he was threadbare
but elegant.


00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:31.641
He always wore spats
and a monocle...


00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:34.848
and I always tell everybody
he treated me


00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:36.604
like I was a mini duchess.


00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:38.762
I'd come in,
and he never seen a kid


00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:40.922
be so interested
in all this junk before.


00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:42.923
I fixed on a brooch.


00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:45.686
I just thought
it was the cat's pajamas,


00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:47.763
and I really lusted
after that piece.


00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:50.810
So I finally saved up 65 cents,


00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:52.405
and we haggled a little bit
over the piece.


00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:53.891
I thought it'd be gone,


00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:56.048
but fortunately for me,
it was still there.


00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:58.771
Anyway, I bought it for
the magnificent sum of 65 cents.


00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:01.930
He gave it to me.
I was so thrilled, my God.


00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:04.607
You comfortable?


00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:06.962
Yeah, fine.
ls it hard for you to do this?


00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:09.327
Is it difficult?


00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:11.005
No, not at all.


00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:13.729
Honey, this is Harlem,
nothing's difficult.


00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:18.242
Now this is...
there's a lot of African stuff,


00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:20.681
- but the jewelry's great, Iris.
- Oh, let me see.


00:13:20.840 --> 00:13:22.922
- Shall we go in? Okay.
- Oh, absolutely.


00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:27.205
See, something white with...


00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:29.169
Yeah, those are all,
like, jackets.


00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:30.560
We'll go around there.


00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:32.844
I've always said that
the population of Harlem


00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:34.565
has much more style
than they do...


00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:36.324
downtown,
they think they're stylish,


00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:38.289
but they all were black,
you know?


00:13:38.440 --> 00:13:41.171
It's not really style.
It's a uniform.


00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:44.324
I used to come up here
on Sundays when I was a kid


00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:46.403
and watch all the ladies
going to church.


00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:47.607
Yeah, Sunday best.


00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:49.768
And they had real style,
I thought.


00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:51.763
And that sort of style
never goes out.


00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:55.242
No, no, but it doesn't exist
much anymore.


00:13:55.400 --> 00:13:57.209
But, you know, things change.


00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:59.442
I hope. I hope.
I try.


00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:06.168
- That's fun.
- The scarf?


00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:07.521
- Yeah.
- Yeah.


00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:09.250
That's like a scarf
or a belt.


00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:11.562
Oh, yeah.
How much?


00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:12.767
That's 45.


00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:14.410
That's a lot.


00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:17.450
Careful, Iris,
you're on "Candid Camera."


00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:19.011
I'm sorry, I'm cheap.


00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:22.403
No! You're not cheap.


00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:25.282
It's funny because
you find people think


00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:28.762
you just acquire things,
but actually, that's not you.


00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:30.251
No, it's hard work.


00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:32.289
You really have to put
a lot of effort into it.


00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:34.647
I know people who have
wonderful collections,


00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:36.404
but it's different.


00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.602
They go out to buy,
they go to...


00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:40.524
Auctions and things
like that, yeah.


00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:43.650
But everything I have,
I go out and find,


00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:45.802
- which is not easy.
- No.


00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:49.407
- I like that.
- That's beautiful.


00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:52.484
I like it.
The color, everything.


00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:54.723
So, monsieur,
how much are these ones?


00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:57.087
Okay, the bracelet ten.


00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.811
What I'll do, I'm going
to make you a deal.


00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.321
We know you, okay?


00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:07.327
Uh, take... I'll give you
$7 each for you.


00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:09.960
And what can you do
on the coat?


00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:13.169
The coat, we sell...
normal, we sell 125.


00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:14.651
But I'll
let you have it 100.


00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:16.962
- Okay.
- I know you.


00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:19.088
I know you, queen.
We know you.


00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:21.447
You can do better than 100.


00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:25.961
- And the bag?
- This 85.


00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:28.327
- Oh, you can do better.
- No, this 85.


00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:31.609
You see, the tanner
took one day to make it.


00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:35.207
- He's slow.
- Yeah. Yeah.


00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:38.082
How did you get that?
I can't get those prices.


00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:39.526
Oh, I'm very good.


00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:42.445
I know.
flaughs)


00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:45.846
Doing something creative,
taking your mind off things,


00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:48.765
but that's how you shop,
and that's how you dress,


00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:50.809
and that's how you think,
in that way.


00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:52.769
Oh, yes,
I mean, it's fun.


00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:55.651
Sometimes I really
don't feel well.


00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:58.326
And I get in there,
it's like I'm 19 1/2.


00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:01.689
I'm jumping around
and everything, and I'm so up.


00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:05.083
When I crawl back to the car...


00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:09.211
- I got my booty...
- How often do you need the fix?


00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:11.687
Oh, I'd like to have it
more often than I get it.


00:16:11.840 --> 00:16:13.205
Like how often, Iris,
once a week?


00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:15.488
- Oh, I would love it once a week.
- Yeah.


00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:22.490
I think what fascinates
me about Iris


00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:26.326
is that she's the perfect
example of the intersection


00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:30.530
of fashion
and interior design and art.


00:16:30.680 --> 00:16:33.843
She's been exposed
to so many different areas


00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:36.207
of creative worlds from being
an interior designer


00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:38.044
to being involved
with Old World Weavers,


00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:41.647
and Old World Weavers,
for those who aren't aware,


00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:46.089
is this iconic company
that Stark then bought,


00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:49.369
but their fabrics are in
some of the finest houses


00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:52.126
that we photograph, you know,
still on a daily basis.


00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:54.402
When we started
Old World Weavers, it came...


00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:55.925
it was a crazy way, too.


00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:57.684
We never intended to go
into the fabric bus...


00:16:57.840 --> 00:16:59.490
Nothing I ever did
I intended to do.


00:16:59.640 --> 00:17:01.404
Everything
just kind of happened.


00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:04.564
She had a very
big decorating business.


00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:06.324
I would go along with her.


00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:09.290
I'd take my little toolbox,
hang the pictures.


00:17:09.440 --> 00:17:11.886
And I got a kick
out of watching her


00:17:12.040 --> 00:17:14.361
make something beautiful.


00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:17.888
One day, I was looking
for a fabric that didn't exist.


00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:20.168
I was doing a house,
and I wanted something special,


00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:23.927
and then I remembered
that I had gone to school


00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:25.969
with a young lady whose father


00:17:26.120 --> 00:17:29.169
was supposed to be
a great weaver.


00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:32.684
And I happened to run into her,
as luck would have it,


00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:36.208
and she said, "Why don't you go
over to the mill and see Papa?"


00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:38.089
So I did, and I brought
the design,


00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:39.930
and he made it,
and it was a big success.


00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:41.610
Everybody liked it.


00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:43.569
And he said did I have
any more designs?


00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:46.451
So after a while,
he asked if Carl and I


00:17:46.600 --> 00:17:49.126
would be interested in going
into business with him.


00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:50.964
And I thought
it would be fun.


00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:56.331
We did exact reproductions
of 17th, 18th, 19th,


00:17:56.480 --> 00:18:00.690
and early 20th Century fabrics,
and I would do my best


00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:04.242
to get them to be as close
as possible to the original.


00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:06.164
That's why we traveled
so much


00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:09.324
because there is no one or two
mills that can do everything.


00:18:09.520 --> 00:18:11.363
It's all very specialized.


00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:14.850
They had done a lot of work
for different administrations


00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:16.525
in the White House
and restored fabrics


00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:18.011
and created special fabrics.


00:18:18.200 --> 00:18:21.807
Well, uh... Barry...


00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:23.530
the White House.


00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:25.523
Was that from Barry...


00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:27.011
We started when Truman
was here.


00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:28.525
I know,
but how did they come...


00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:30.444
I don't know,
they heard about us.


00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:33.365
You see, the White House
has a fine arts commission.


00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:35.841
Well, now,
can you make these changes


00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:39.049
according to your own
personal tastes and desires?


00:18:39.200 --> 00:18:42.204
Well, no,
I have a committee...


00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:44.931
Oh, we had a problem
with Jackie.


00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:47.048
Stop.


00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:49.089
We're not supposed to talk
too much about the White House.


00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:51.561
They get very upset.


00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.121
My God, we did
so many restorations


00:18:54.320 --> 00:18:56.163
in so many different museums.


00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:58.840
I remember
I did Stephen Foster's


00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:00.843
- My Old Kentucky Home.
- Did the Senate.


00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:03.048
I did the Statehouse
in Mississippi.


00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:06.084
Every great house
in America had our stuff.


00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:08.208
I had a very
interesting clientele.


00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:10.442
They didn't want to have things
that everybody else had,


00:19:10.600 --> 00:19:13.126
so that started me traveling,


00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:15.123
and I went twice a year
to Europe.


00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:16.691
I was a busy bee.


00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:45.286
And I would wear
three cameras.


00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:47.727
I had a Nikon with a telly,


00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:52.329
a Nikon normal,
and a Nikon wide-angle.


00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:56.201
And the situations
would come up so fast,


00:19:56.360 --> 00:19:59.489
I didn't have time
to change lenses.


00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:02.291
So it was easier to have
the three cameras.


00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:05.090
J' Hey, hey,
la la la la la J'


00:20:05.240 --> 00:20:11.327
J' Hey, hey,
la la la la la J'


00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:12.851
♪ Hey, hey <i>I</i>


00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:16.684
When you'd get to know
some of the dealers like we did,


00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:21.164
they'd open up places for us
like you never saw before.


00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:22.885
They open at a certain hour,


00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.804
and they close
at a certain hour.


00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:28.089
Who do you think
was the last one? Iris.


00:20:33.440 --> 00:20:34.771
I don't like to haggle
too much,


00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:36.610
and I don't like to haggle
like some people do.


00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:39.491
You have to know where to haggle
and when to haggle.


00:20:39.640 --> 00:20:41.802
There are certain places
in the world


00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:43.962
where you do a disservice
to the merchant


00:20:44.120 --> 00:20:45.451
if you don't haggle.


00:20:45.640 --> 00:20:47.881
If he says,
"This piece is $50,"


00:20:48.040 --> 00:20:50.964
and you give him $50,
you have totally ruined his day


00:20:51.120 --> 00:20:53.600
because he thinks, oh, my God,
if you're stupid enough


00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:55.888
to give me $50,
right off the bat,


00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:57.929
I should have asked you 150.


00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:00.003
So it's part of the game.


00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:08.443
J' Hey, hey,
la la la la la J'


00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:09.840
♪ Hey, hey <i>I</i>


00:21:13.680 --> 00:21:15.842
I didn't want to sell it,
and finally,


00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:18.441
Starks made an offer
we couldn't refuse,


00:21:18.600 --> 00:21:21.809
and I got more into all this
nutty stuff I'm doing now...


00:21:23.160 --> 00:21:25.242
which is more fun.


00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:32.045
Oh, I love this.


00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:33.964
Here, let me show you.


00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:36.608
It's great.
I have some weird jewelry.


00:21:36.760 --> 00:21:39.161
Oh, they're wonderful.
Oh.


00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:41.524
Oh, my goodness.


00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:46.001
Okay, you have
a few sips of tea,


00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:49.204
and then I'm going to
start my grooming.


00:21:53.680 --> 00:21:55.125
Well.


00:21:57.400 --> 00:21:59.971
Oh, thank you.
I need a Scotch after this.


00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:02.248
Flaughs)
ls it tight or is it okay?


00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:05.529
No, no, no, it'sjust
I look like a ghoul.


00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:07.484
- You look amazing.
- Oh, come on.


00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:27.084
Beautiful, like that.
Let me see you smile.


00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:29.567
There you go, okay, one, two,
three, don't move.


00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:30.960
Love it.


00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:32.730
Now put your...
yeah, now that one.


00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:34.769
Yeah, beautiful, I love that.
That's perfect.


00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:36.763
There you go, yeah.
I love you right there.


00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:39.127
Stay like that.
Now smile, again, a big smile.


00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:42.409
One, two, three, great.


00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:45.683
- This is fun.
- What?


00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:47.530
- This is fun.
- It is.


00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:55.128
Okay, one, two...
eyes just a little bit...


00:22:55.280 --> 00:22:56.805
Keep your face there
and your eyes... there you go.


00:22:56.960 --> 00:22:58.769
One, two, three.
Awesome.


00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:00.763
All right, thank you very much,
Iris.


00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:03.810
Yay.
uaughs)


00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:08.562
Have you ever thought
about having plastic surgery?


00:23:08.720 --> 00:23:11.371
Oh, God, no.
Oh, I'm very opposed to that.


00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:14.683
I think unless God gave you
a nose like Pinocchio


00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:18.447
or you were in a fire or some
terrible thing, why mess?


00:23:18.600 --> 00:23:21.080
Because you don't know
how it's going to come out.


00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:24.480
You could come out worse
than when you started.


00:23:24.640 --> 00:23:26.881
I mean, some very important
people I know go...


00:23:27.040 --> 00:23:29.441
They come out
looking like Picasso.


00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:32.251
It's not...
Why?


00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:34.448
Why do you want to do it for,
a few wrinkles?


00:23:34.640 --> 00:23:36.563
I see no reason for it.


00:23:38.440 --> 00:23:40.044
I mean, maybe people
in show business


00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:42.567
who are obliged
to look young or something,


00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:44.563
but everybody knows
how old you are,


00:23:44.720 --> 00:23:46.449
and then you have the
scrawny old hands


00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:48.887
that don't go with your face,


00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:51.964
and some people start
to fall down.


00:23:52.120 --> 00:23:54.168
L... No.


00:24:39.240 --> 00:24:42.005
I first met Iris
when a friend


00:24:42.160 --> 00:24:45.960
who is an appraiser said,


00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:48.361
"l've just met a woman
with the most remarkable


00:24:48.520 --> 00:24:51.603
collection
of couture costume jewelry.


00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:55.327
She probably has
one of the two best collections


00:24:55.480 --> 00:24:56.527
in the United States."


00:24:56.720 --> 00:24:58.131
That was the beginning.


00:24:58.280 --> 00:25:00.009
I was working at F.l.T.
at the time,


00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:04.449
and she was one of the great
lenders to our exhibitions


00:25:04.600 --> 00:25:07.331
at F.l.T., the Fashion Institute
of Technology.


00:25:07.520 --> 00:25:10.603
So we became friends,
and I saw her socially,


00:25:10.760 --> 00:25:12.683
and even after we started
at the Met,


00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:15.366
there was occasion
to borrow from her.


00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:20.731
We had a show that was planned,
and at the very last minute,


00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:23.611
there was something that
happened that it couldn't be...


00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:26.161
We couldn't proceed,
and it was very late.


00:25:26.320 --> 00:25:28.402
It was in the early summer.


00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:30.403
And I thought, well,


00:25:30.560 --> 00:25:32.847
we have to do a show
for the fall,


00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:34.604
and what could it be?


00:25:34.800 --> 00:25:38.441
I thought, you know, Iris
has this renowned collection


00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:42.611
of costume jewelry that'll
be relatively easy to set up.


00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:44.205
Why don't we do that?


00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:46.328
As I was talking to her,
it occurred to me


00:25:46.520 --> 00:25:48.568
we could do
something really fun.


00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:50.961
Because instead
of having the jewelry


00:25:51.120 --> 00:25:54.966
on these abstract mounts,
what we could do


00:25:55.120 --> 00:25:58.329
is style them in a way
that they were Iris.


00:25:58.480 --> 00:26:02.201
He said, "l think it would make
a much more interesting show


00:26:02.360 --> 00:26:03.930
if you could show
the public


00:26:04.120 --> 00:26:05.724
what you can do
with accessories.


00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:08.565
How they can transform
an outfit.


00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:13.169
Could you possibly spare
about five outfits?


00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:17.245
You would have 100% authority
to accessorize them


00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:18.970
any way you saw fit."


00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:20.485
So I said, "Oh,
that sounds like fun.


00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:22.409
What would you like?"


00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:25.206
"Well, we don't know.
What have you got?"


00:26:25.360 --> 00:26:27.601
And that opened
the Pandora's box.


00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:33.761
When you think
about Iris' collection,


00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:35.843
several rooms in her
Park Avenue apartment


00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:37.604
are dedicated to her clothes.


00:26:37.760 --> 00:26:39.285
Her closets
are double height.


00:26:39.440 --> 00:26:43.684
That means two levels
of clothing jammed together


00:26:43.840 --> 00:26:46.366
where you'll just see a sliver
of a Saint Laurent skirt


00:26:46.520 --> 00:26:47.760
or an Ungaro blouse.


00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:49.610
Lagerfeld piece.


00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:51.285
Dior.


00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:52.766
Galanos.


00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:54.166
This is a shaman...


00:26:54.320 --> 00:26:58.723
It's a Chinese shaman's...
jacket


00:26:58.880 --> 00:27:00.530
But I'll wear it
as a cocktail outfit


00:27:00.720 --> 00:27:01.960
with skinny pants.


00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:04.248
She had
her mother's apartment,


00:27:04.400 --> 00:27:06.528
which was filled
with clothing.


00:27:06.680 --> 00:27:09.331
She had her apartment
in Palm Beach,


00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:11.409
which was filled with clothing.


00:27:11.560 --> 00:27:14.643
And so even for the person
who's put it together,


00:27:14.800 --> 00:27:19.567
you know, what point
do you select this over that?


00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:23.051
We knew that this
was going to be a show


00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:25.686
that looked like nothing else
we had ever done.


00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:27.251
They were very much afraid


00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:31.121
because they had never done
a one-woman show ever.


00:27:31.280 --> 00:27:33.044
It was like a written law.


00:27:33.200 --> 00:27:35.248
If you're one-woman show
and you were not in the trade,


00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:36.640
you ought to be dead.


00:27:36.840 --> 00:27:38.410
Because
the previous exhibition


00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:41.325
had fallen apart
and there was no sponsorship,


00:27:41.480 --> 00:27:43.164
we had to make it
very close to the vest.


00:27:43.320 --> 00:27:45.641
There was not
a lot of publicity.


00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:47.086
It would have to be
word-of-mouth.


00:27:47.240 --> 00:27:50.005
We didn't expect
that it would be


00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:52.288
quite the phenomenon
that it was.


00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:02.486
I don't know if there's been
any other show


00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:04.802
that has relied so much
on word-of-mouth.


00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:06.405
That's what surprised us,


00:28:06.560 --> 00:28:08.767
people coming in
and leaving and saying,


00:28:08.920 --> 00:28:10.251
"You have to see this show."


00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:12.129
The response was remarkable.


00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:21.401
I went back numerous times.
It was absolutely fascinating.


00:28:21.560 --> 00:28:24.291
And then I'd overhear people...
the comments people made.


00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:25.487
On a rare occasion,


00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:27.728
I would comment
on their comments.


00:28:27.880 --> 00:28:30.451
He'd tell me that people
would say, you know,


00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:32.087
"Well, who is she?"


00:28:32.160 --> 00:28:33.650
And they'd say, "Oh,
I don't know, she's dead."


00:28:33.800 --> 00:28:35.962
So I told them,
"The next time you hear that,


00:28:36.120 --> 00:28:38.646
tap the lady on the shoulder
and say,


